+## Application typechecking
+
+The CLI uses its pinned `tsrs` dependency for application typechecks during
+`dev`, `deploy`, `codegen`, and `typecheck`. There is no compiler selector:
+remove `typescriptCompiler` from `convex.json`, and do not pass
+`--typescript-compiler`. No separate installation of tsrs is needed. With
+`--typecheck=enable`, a tsconfig is required; `try` skips projects without one;
+`disable` skips checking. A missing or unusable installed compiler fails a
+configured check in both enable and try modes. Checks always use `--noEmit`;
+JavaScript bundling is unchanged.
+
+tsrs 0.18 uses TypeScript 7.1 development semantics and supports Linux x64/arm64
+and macOS arm64. Review your project's TypeScript configuration and diagnostics
+when upgrading. Unsupported hosts cannot run these application typechecks. The
+SDK also uses tsrs to build its public and internal declarations, preserving the
+ESM and CommonJS package layouts and declaration compatibility with older
+TypeScript consumers.
+
+This does not impose a backend protocol requirement. Clients using an older
+convex-js release and tsc can still deploy to the updated backend.
 
